<title>USA: U.S. Nov personal income rose 0.5 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. Nov personal income rose 0.5 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1996-12-23</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Commerce Department personal income and spending estimates, in seasonally adjusted annual rates.</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:    Nov    Oct--(Prev)  Sept--(Prev)   Aug</p>
<p> Income (Current Dlrs)  0.5   Unch   Unch    0.6    0.6    0.6</p>
<p>Disposable Income:</p>
<p> -- Current Dlrs	  0.5    0.1   Unch    0.5    0.6    0.5</p>
<p> -- Chain '92 Dlrs	0.3   -0.2   -0.3    0.3    0.4    0.5</p>
<p>Consumption:</p>
<p> -- Current Dlrs	  0.5    0.7    0.5    0.1    0.1    0.5</p>
<p> -- Chain '92 Dlrs	0.3    0.4    0.2   -0.1   -0.1    0.4</p>
<p>    Billions of Dlrs:   Nov    Oct--(Prev)  Sept--(Prev)   Aug</p>
<p> Income		   6,574  6,542  6,544  6,539  6,542  6,503</p>
<p> Disposable Income    5,691  5,662  5,662  5,658  5,659  5,629</p>
<p> Personal Outlays     5,406  5,377  5,367  5,340  5,339  5,337</p>
<p> Pers.Consumption     5,241  5,212  5,202  5,175  5,175  5,172</p>
<p> -Durable Goods	   637    633    629    624    627    643</p>
<p> -Non-Durable Goods   1,562  1,559  1,557  1,553  1,552  1,543</p>
<p> -Services		3,042  3,020  3,017  2,999  2,996  2,987</p>
<p> Personal Savings	 285    285    295    319    320    293</p>
<p> Pct Savings Rate	 5.0    5.0    5.2    5.6    5.7    5.2</p>
<p>    Billions of Dlrs:   Nov    Oct--(Prev)  Sept--(Prev)   Aug</p>
<p> Wages and Salaries   3,704  3,682  3,685  3,687  3,692  3,661</p>
<p> -Manufacturing	   683    680    679    680    680    681</p>
<p>-Service Industries  1,298  1,285  1,284  1,286  1,288  1,271</p>
<p> -Government		646    647    649    647    647    644</p>
<p> Proprietors' Income    530    529    528    530    530    526</p>
<p> -Farm			 49     51     51     53     53     52</p>
<p> -Non-Farm		  481    479    477    477    477    474</p>
<p>Wall Street economists expected November personal income to rise 0.6 percent and spending to be up 0.3 percent.</p>
<p>The department said gains in November employment, average weekly hours and average hourly earnings contributed to the rise in personal income last month.</p>
<p>The revised October spending increase of 0.7 percent was the highest since a 0.8 percent rise in May, the department said. October spending was previously reported up 0.5 percent.</p>
